> We have a small sample (N=200), items with floor and ceiling effects (nonnormal distribution). Which estimator do you recommend for CFA analysis?

I agree with João, but would add that Bayesian SEM could be an option.  You can specify custom priors in blavaan, but I think you can specify a custom likelihood.  You would need to specify that the Stan or JAGS script be saved, then edit it manually to specify a truncated distribution for the likelihood (assuming that is a reasonable assumption of your data).  It is a pretty advanced method requiring some programming savvy, but you could ask on SEMNET whether anyone can recommend articles that illustrate how to fit BSEMs to truncated or censored data.

https://listserv.ua.edu/cgi-bin/wa?SUBED1=semnet&A=1

> With WLSM no robust CFI and RMSEA values are reported, do you know why?

https://doi.org/10.1080/00273171.2018.1455142

